<GuideLayout 
  title="Seamless vs Cut-and-Sew: Choosing the Right Construction"
  type="CONSTRUCTION"
  description="Deep dive into manufacturing techniques. Understand which construction method suits your design, budget, and target market."
>
  <h2>The Engineering Behind the Fit</h2>
  <p>The construction method defines the look, feel, and cost of your activewear. Choosing the right one is essential for meeting your brand's performance and price goals.</p>
  
  <h3>Cut-and-Sew Manufacturing</h3>
  <p>This is the traditional method where fabric panels are cut from a roll and sewn together.
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Unlimited design versatility, easier to mix fabrics/colors, and lower machinery costs.</li>
      <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Seams can sometimes cause chafing if not finished correctly (e.g., without flatlock stitching).</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <h3>Seamless Manufacturing</h3>
  <p>Seamless garments are knitted as a continuous tube on specialized circular knitting machines.
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pros:</strong> Maximum comfort, "second-skin" feel, and high technical appeal.</li>
      <li><strong>Cons:</strong> Limited color/pattern complexity compared to cut-and-sew, and higher development costs for specific sizes.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <h3>Which should you choose?</h3>
  <p>For high-intensity yoga and gym wear where comfort is king, <strong>Seamless</strong> is often the winner. For fashion-forward activewear with complex paneling and color blocking, <strong>Cut-and-Sew</strong> is the way to go.</p>
</GuideLayout>
